  "final_text": "All four steps are done, and the read-back shows the rule enabled.\n\n- **Data view:** `Kibana Sample Data Logs` uses the time field `timestamp`. The response code is the `response` field, a string, so I used `response.keyword`.\n- **Dashboard:** I created `S4-bb82fd0f Web logs` with two panels. \"Requests over time\" is a line chart of request counts by `timestamp`. The second panel is a bar chart of request counts by response code.\n- **Alert rule:** `S4-bb82fd0f 5xx errors` is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It runs every minute and fires when more than 10 requests have `response.keyword` from \"500\" to \"599\" within 5 minutes. It was created disabled, so I enabled it.\n- **Panel edit:** I renamed the response-code panel to \"Responses by code\". The dashboard read-back shows the new title.\n- **Read-back:** The rule shows `enabled: true`. Its status is still \"pending\" because it hasn't run yet.\n\nThe 500\u2013599 range compares the codes as text, which works because every code is three digits.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
